  "summary": "San Francisco's moderate Democratic establishment is defending SFPD's use of Flock cameras despite a national backlash and revelations that out-of-state agencies illegally accessed the city's ALPR data 1.6 million times. The local Democratic Party's central committee approved resolutions supporting continued use of license-plate readers and speed cameras, defeating former Supervisor Gordon Mar's resolution to suspend Flock until federal/out-of-state access can be cut off. City Hall is separately weighing additional data-use guardrails.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "A September 2025 Standard investigation found that San Francisco's Flock license-plate database was illegally accessed 1.6 million times by out-of-state agencies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "It defeated a resolution from former Supervisor Gordon Mar that called for suspending the use of Flock cameras until the SFPD can prove it's able to cut off camera access to out-of-state and federal law enforcement agencies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 29
    },
    {
      "quote": "In February, officials revealed that an SFPD officer used the cameras to search for his wife's stolen car, in violation of department rules.",
      "paragraph_idx": 32
    },
    {
      "quote": "You cannot be a paid advocate for a surveillance product and be a neutral arbiter of its oversight",
      "paragraph_idx": 27
    }
  ],
